The {{nihongo|'''Tokuyama Line'''|徳山線|Tokuyama-sen}} is a subway line in [[Okami|Okami (大紙)]], [[Okaiken]], operated by [[Okami Metro]]. It serves as a direct southern connector, linking the commercial district of [[Kuromon-Bashi]] with the southern residential areas along the Yamato River, acting as the gateway to [[Tokuyama Prefecture]]. On system maps, the line is color-coded jade ({{Okami Metro color|D}}), and its stations use the prefix "D".

Designated as Line 6, the Tokuyama Line serves a specific role as a bypass and feeder line. Unlike the third-rail lines in the city center, it utilizes overhead catenary electrification to facilitate seamless reciprocal through-service with the [[Tokutetsu|Tokutetsu (Tokuyama Electric Railway)]]. This allows passengers to travel from the industrial centers of Tokuyama directly into the heart of Okami's electronics district without changing trains.



==History==
Construction of the Tokuyama Line (Line 6) began in the late 1960s to support the rapid industrialization of the southern districts. The line opened on October 1, 1969, making it one of the earlier lines in the network, established to manage the heavy commuter flow from the [[Tokutetsu]] railway.

The line was built to accommodate 6-car trains from the outset, matching the standard formation of the private railway partner. While originally planned to extend further north, the construction of the [[Sakaisuji Line]] (now the [[Asahi Line]]) made further northern extension redundant, cementing Kuromon-Bashi as its definitive northern terminal.

